// Provider Explorer -- who sells compute, and what a person could rent out for $TRI.
//
// The seventh step of the site's ladder: Specs → Skills → Crons → Agents → Tools →
// Functions → Providers. A provider card is generated by scripts/agents-from-specs.mjs
// from specs/providers/<network>/<slug>.t27 through the real compiler and checked
// against the schema in specs/providers/catalog.t27. Two families: a model the Gonka
// chain lists (every number copied from a public chain endpoint on the card's CHECKED
// date), and a class of hardware a person could rent out to TRI-NET for $TRI (an
// Artix-7 FPGA measured on three boards, a 24 GB gaming GPU that is a design only).
// specs/providers/tri_gnk_pair.t27 is the study under both: how Gonka pays its hosts,
// the routes a $TRI/GNK pair could take, and where the two networks differ.
//
// Three things this page never does. It never calls a model: every Gonka inference is
// paid and signed (catalog.t27 CALL_RULE), so it shows the request and sends nothing.
// It never asks for a key or a wallet. And it never shows a Gonka host's address: the
// host count is read from the epoch's participants and nothing else of them is kept
// (lib/gonkaLive). The chain is re-read only when the reader asks, from the reader's
// browser, and every distance between a card and the chain is printed, not smoothed.

